WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:15.590
INTRO:
Toyota was one of the first automakers to

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:17.330
embrace an EV future.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:21.890
I mean, how could anyone forget the iconic
Toyota Prius.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:27.550
The Prius didn’t have the best of social
images, but it was and still is one of the

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:30.010
most cost effective vehicles on the road.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:36.440
Aside from pioneering the way with hybrids,
Toyota was also an early investor in Tesla.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:43.820
Way back in 2010, Toyota invested $50 million
into Tesla which gave them a 1.43% stake in

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.020
the company.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:51.660
If they were still holding this stake, it
would be worth over $10 billion, but unfortunately,

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.149
they sold this stake in mid 2017.

00:00:54.149 --> 00:01:00.300
At the time, it seemed like Toyota was simply
taking risk off the table and locking in profits,

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:05.260
but looking back, it seems like something
changed with Toyota as they sold off this

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:06.260
stake.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:10.979
Since then, Toyota has actually become more
of an EV opposer.

00:01:10.979 --> 00:01:16.060
For example, Toyota has actually been lobbying
to slow down the move to EVs.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:20.470
They’ve also been behind the curve when
it comes to fully electric vehicles.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:25.960
It wasn’t till April of 2022 that they released
their first fully electric SUV onto the market.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:31.430
And they’ve actually recalled this vehicle
due the wheels falling off, so it’s not

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:33.080
exactly going great.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:37.940
Now, supporters of Toyota would probably point
out that Toyota is investing a significant

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:39.390
amount into battery technology.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:46.240
For instance, they have a plan to invest $13.5
billion into battery tech by the end of the

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:47.240
decade.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.130
But, compared to the rest of the industry,
this is rather underwhelming.

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:52.310
Take Ford for example.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:57.420
Ford is literally drowning in debt with over
$200 billion in liabilities.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:04.190
Yet, they just increased their EV investment
from $30 billion through 2026 to $50 billion.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:11.150
Similarly, Volkswagen is investing $180 billion
into EVs over the next 4 years.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:15.250
Considering these investments, Toyota’s
commitment is rather underwhelming.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:21.650
Clearly, they haven’t ditched EVs altogether,
but at the same time, they’re also refusing

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.060
to go all in and jump onto the EV bandwagon.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:30.900
So, why is Toyota so hesitant with EVs?

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:36.519
HYDROGEN:
One of the main reasons Toyota has been so

00:02:36.519 --> 00:02:41.230
hesitant when it comes to EVs is because they’ve
been betting on hydrogen.

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:45.620
According to Toyota’s Vice chairman, they
“don’t want to be seen as an EV maker,

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:48.250
but as a carbon-neutral company.”

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:53.700
Toyota argues that this carbon neutral future
does not have to be achieved with EVs.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:58.620
“Carbon neutrality is not about one having
a single choice, but about keeping options

00:02:58.620 --> 00:02:59.650
open.”

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:05.020
This in itself is not a bad stance per se,
but thus far, we’ve only seen issues when

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:07.010
it comes to hydrogen vehicles.

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:09.500
Take the 2021 Tokyo Olympics for example.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.989
Toyota supplied the Olympics with 500 hydrogen
busses and cars.

00:03:13.989 --> 00:03:20.110
The vehicles themselves worked alright, but
the same could not be said about the finances.

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:24.690
You see, Toyota leased out these busses to
Japan for $900,000 for 6 years.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:29.340
A comparable diesel bus could have been leased
out for $220,000 for 15 years.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:35.099
On an annual basis, the hydrogen bus works
out to $150,000 per year while the diesel

00:03:35.099 --> 00:03:38.380
bus works out to under $15,000 per year.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:44.860
In other words, the hydrogen option is literally
10 times as expensive, and that’s just the

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:45.860
lease.

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:49.670
Unlike EVs where fueling up is dramatically
cheaper than gas counterparts, with these

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:53.900
hydrogen busses, it’s actually 2.6 times
more expensive.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:59.239
If you thought EVs were already expensive,
these are clearly on a different level.

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:04.280
To make things worse, financial concerns are
just the first of your worries.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:08.500
Hydrogen vehicles also suffer from an extremely
underdeveloped refueling network.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:12.519
Unlike electric charging, you can’t just
setup a few charging stations that link up

00:04:12.519 --> 00:04:14.079
to the local power grid.

00:04:14.079 --> 00:04:18.860
Instead, you have to buy hydrogen specific
fuel dispensers that often cost $100,000 per

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:19.860
unit.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:24.240
You also have to spend a significant amount
on storing the hydrogen and you also have

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.780
to deal with regular hydrogen deliveries.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:30.169
In other words, you basically have to run
a gas station.

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:34.169
And given that the majority of gas stations
make their money off of the convenience stores

00:04:34.169 --> 00:04:38.500
attached to the gas stations, I don’t think
they’ll be particularly thrilled to invest

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.460
into hydrogen infrastructure.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:44.680
One advantage that should be pointed out,
however, is that filling up a hydrogen vehicle

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:49.120
is similar to filling up a gas vehicle meaning
that you can get a full tank of gas within

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.770
just 3 to 5 minutes.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:55.860
If you’re someone who does a lot of road
trips, this is likely a significant advantage,

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.630
but that’s pretty much where the advantages
end.

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:02.990
It’s not just the fueling process that’s
similar between gas vehicles and hydrogen

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:03.990
vehicles.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:06.780
The maintenance process is also quite similar.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:11.800
You see, hydrogen vehicles are way more complex
than EVs meaning that they naturally come

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:15.110
along with more maintenance given that more
things can go wrong.

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:17.156
So, from a big picture view, hydrogen vehicles
cost more, they require more maintenance,

00:05:17.156 --> 00:05:18.490
and the infrastructure is the least developed,
so hydrogen vehicles just don’t stack up

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:20.120
against EVs.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:23.830
But then why is Toyota so determined to go
down this path?

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:28.370
Well, the answer seems to lie in what’s
best for Japan as opposed to what’s best

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:29.610
for everyone.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:36.410
JAPAN’S ENERGY CRISIS:
Japan’s obsession with hydrogen is nothing

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:37.410
new.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:43.229
In fact, it’s just one of many avenues that
Japan has been experimenting with for decades.

00:05:43.229 --> 00:05:48.629
And the main reason the country doesn’t
want to bend to western standards is because

00:05:48.629 --> 00:05:51.020
of the country’s energy dependence.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:56.910
Japan is basically just an island which means
that they don’t have any neighboring countries.

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:59.490
At first glance, this might seem cool.

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:02.740
They have this entire piece of land all to
themselves.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:06.509
But, logistically, this is actually a nightmare.

00:06:06.509 --> 00:06:11.409
Here’s the thing, when you aren’t physically
attached to any other countries, shipping

00:06:11.409 --> 00:06:16.350
becomes extremely difficult because everything
has to be shipping through the sea.

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:20.720
And this is especially a problem when we’re
talking about energy.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:25.370
You see, much of the world either creates
their own energy or they rely on neighboring

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:26.600
countries.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:31.409
Europe for example used to rely on Russia
for crude oil and natural gas.

00:06:31.409 --> 00:06:35.910
They had a massive set of pipelines that facilitated
the trades between the two regions.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:39.220
A similar case can be seen with America as
well.

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:44.540
Canada is the largest energy exporter to America
and the reasoning is obvious.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:48.440
The two countries are touching which makes
it easy to trade energy.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:52.139
Japan obviously doesn't have any such partners.

00:06:52.139 --> 00:06:57.620
They also don’t have any of the high demand
resources like crude oil or natural gas.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:02.280
So, they’ve essentially been forced into
importing all of their energy.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:07.389
This is not only expensive, but it also puts
Japan at a massive trading disadvantage.

00:07:07.389 --> 00:07:13.729
I mean, when you’re importing 94% of your
energy, you’re not exactly in the best position

00:07:13.729 --> 00:07:16.560
to implement tariffs or start a trade war.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:20.240
The other countries could hit back with an
energy boycott, and you’re country would

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:22.409
be screwed within a matter of days.

00:07:22.409 --> 00:07:28.289
Japan is obviously not a fan of this situation
and they’ve been trying to escape this situation

00:07:28.289 --> 00:07:29.289
for decades.

00:07:29.289 --> 00:07:33.699
Their initial solution to this conundrum was
going all in on nuclear energy and this was

00:07:33.699 --> 00:07:35.930
actually playing out really well.

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:41.129
At their peak in 2010, they were able to produce
13% of the entire country’s energy needs

00:07:41.129 --> 00:07:42.490
using nuclear power.

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:48.380
But, all of this would come crashing down
in 2011 with the Fukushima Nuclear disaster.

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:51.040
You probably heard about this on the news.

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:56.699
154,000 people had to be evacuted and it’s
ranked as the second worst nuclear disaster

00:07:56.699 --> 00:08:00.240
of all time, only beaten out by Chernobyl.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:04.449
The worst part is that Japan couldn’t even
do anything about it.

00:08:04.449 --> 00:08:08.680
It’s not like the disaster was caused mismanagement
or negligence.

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:12.729
It was actually caused by a record breaking
earthquake plus tsunami combination.

00:08:12.729 --> 00:08:17.900
Really, the only way Japan could’ve avoided
this disaster was to not support nuclear energy

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:19.789
in the first place.

00:08:19.789 --> 00:08:24.699
And that’s exactly what they’ve been trying
to do eversince, but this has catapulted the

00:08:24.699 --> 00:08:27.879
entire country into a perpetual energy crisis.

00:08:27.879 --> 00:08:32.440
Nowadays, its extremely common for Japan to
issue power conservation messages to households

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:37.280
and companies as the country is constantly
fighting to avoid blackouts.

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:42.539
As you would guess, Japan has been frantically
looking for solutions eversince the disaster,

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:46.540
and one of the top solutions that they’ve
landed on is hydrogen.

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:52.570
A SAVIOR:
While Japan doesn’t have access to any of

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:57.140
the common sources of energy, what they do
have access to is Hydrogen.

00:08:57.140 --> 00:09:01.210
For once, Japan’s ocean surroundings are
actually helpful.

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:06.040
You see, the ocean floor surrounding Japan
is filled with a sherbert like substance often

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:08.670
referred to as “fire ice”.

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:13.230
These ice crystals hold a tremendous amount
of natural methane which can be converted

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:15.320
into even more hydrogen.

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:20.750
It’s estimated that just one cubic meter
of methane hydrate can produce 160 cubic meters

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:24.540
of methane gas which can then be turned into
hydrogen.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:29.060
And given that Japan has an exclusive economic
zone that stretches to both sides of the country,

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:31.790
they have access to plenty of “fire ice”.

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:37.860
Now, it’s not all sunshines and rainbows
given that it’s not easy to extract “fire

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:38.860
ice”.

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:42.780
First, you have to voyage out deep into the
ocean where the sea floor is a 1000 meters

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:43.960
under the surface.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:47.960
From there, you have to drill hundreds of
meters into the sea bed to actually retrieve

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:49.100
the “fire ice”.

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:53.430
Japan has been working on a new drilling method
that can potentially extract “fire ice”

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:58.240
from shallower locations and then send them
through underwater pipelines, but this is

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:00.040
still a work in progress.

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:04.550
It’s not clear if this new process will
work out, but it doesn’t matter whether

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:06.420
it does or not.

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:11.990
For the first time in a long time, Japan actually
has an alternative form of energy.

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:16.500
And if this process doesn’t work out, they’re
just gonna keep trying different processes

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:18.930
until they figure out something that does
work.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:23.920
For them, this is not a matter of, “oh let’s
switch renewable energy because it’s the

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:26.510
right thing to do and it’s trendy”.

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:31.590
No, for them, this is a matter of “we have
to figure this out ASAP if we want to save

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:33.250
Japan from the energy crisis.”

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:39.640
So, they’ve obviously taking this extremely
seriously, and as Japan’s largest company,

00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:43.340
Toyota is obviously doing everything they
can to support this vision.

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:47.820
And yes, that includes lobbying other countries
to slow down EV adoption.

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:52.420
When you look at it from the western perspective,
it seems like Toyota are the bad guys who

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:56.570
are teaming up with the oil giants like Shell
and Exxon to stall this wholesome movement

00:10:56.570 --> 00:11:00.990
towards EVs, but that’s not really the case
at all.

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:05.830
Toyota isn’t against renewable energy or
EVs, they’re simply trying to promote the

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:10.410
form of energy that would be most beneficial
for their own country.

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:16.530
ONE SHOT:
Global energy changes don’t happen often.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:19.620
If anything, they might happen once a century.

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:25.270
So, we’re truly living through historic
times, and Japan only has one shot at getting

00:11:25.270 --> 00:11:26.270
this right.

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:31.210
Either they convince the world that hydrogen
is worth perusing right now, or they get left

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:34.640
behind as the entire world switches to EVs.

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:38.800
If this happens, Japan would just become even
more dependent on the rest of the world for

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:39.800
energy imports.

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:44.010
Also, allocating a significant portion of
their electricity towards running electric

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:47.830
cars will simply increase their chances of
a blackout even more.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:52.010
So, that’s the last thing Japan wants to
do.

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:55.990
Something else that we should note is that
Japan is not even the country that would benefit

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:57.900
most from a switch to hydrogen.

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:01.960
It’s not like Japan has so much hydrogen
that they would become the next Saudi Arabia

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:02.960
or something.

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:07.990
Rather, a trend towards hydrogen would simply
alleviate their dependency for energy.

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:11.960
The country that would really profit from
this shift is China who is the leader in hydrogen

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:13.680
production and consumption.

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:19.450
China’s current hydrogen usage comes in
at a whopping 24 million tonnes per year.

00:12:19.450 --> 00:12:24.420
Now, it should be noted that most of this
hydrogen is “grey” hydrogen meaning that

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:26.110
it’s produced using fossil fuels.

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:31.310
But, China is setting up loads of “green”
hydrogen plants as we speak, so they would

00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:34.470
be the ones that would benefit most from such
a transition.

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:39.710
So, in the end, why is Toyota refusing to
jump onto the EV bandwagon despite pioneering

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:40.810
the industry?

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:46.480
Well, their home country, Japan has been struggling
with energy for decades, and there has been

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:48.480
a glimmer of hope with hydrogen.

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:52.670
So, they’re trying their best to leverage
this opportunity and maximize the benefit

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:57.370
for Japan, but only time will tell if this
gamble will be worth it.

00:12:57.370 --> 00:13:02.300
Now that you see it from Japan’s perspective,
do you think Toyota is doing the wrong thing?

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:03.300
Comment that down below.

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:07.920
Also, drop a like if you hope that Japan is
able to overcome their energy crisis.

